# Introduction

@<introduction of litar@=
Programmers have a lot of text to deal with. They write code,
they write code to compile code, they write document, they write
code to format document, they write code to parse whatever text,
they even spell commands to control computer through shells.
Basically, all work that programmers do is writing text.

The traditional unit of text is file, text of different types
is saved in seperated files, but sometimes, it might take
too much energy to create a new file. Like, you want to
insert a build time information to your code whenever you build
your program. To do that, you might write a simple sed script and
put that to Makefile. Here is the problem, sed operate on the whole
file, so you have to make sure that the sed script won't influence the other
parts of the file. To solve that, you might seperate that part of code to a new file,
use a function to encapsulate it or simply store the information
in a macro or a variable and pass back to the original file.
You can see all the extra efforts made here.

litar is created to operate on a smaller unit of text, which is called chunk.
With litar, you can gather text of whatever type to a single file
and describe the relations between chunks at ease. Some relations
are hard or cumbersome to implement if between files.
What's more, follow the spirit of literate programming,
the readability is supposed to be improved a lot.
A such file is called a litar archive, the name litar is by combining
literate and archive.
@

@<syntax of litar@=
Because a litar archive is supposed to contain text of all types, the syntax of
itself should be simple. The syntax of a litar archive is built upon
control characters, which are @@ followed by a character.

To fully describe all concepts, we shall introduce another term, block.
In the outermost, there are blocks interleaved with comments.
Each block consists of two parts: the name of a chunk and contents of current block.

```
Some comments.

@@<chunk1@@=
    The contents of block1.
@@

Some other comments

@@<chunk2@@=
    The contents of block2.
@@

@@<chunk1@@=
    The contents of block3.
@@

```

We say here that chunk1 is extended by block1 and block3, chunk2 is extended by block2.

There is another relation between chunk and block, include.

```
Some comments.

@@<chunk1@@=
    The contents of block1.
@@

Some other comments

@@<chunk2@@=
    The contents of block2.
    @@<chunk1@@>
    The contents of block2.
@@

```

The contents of block2 contains a reference to chunk1, we say block2 includes chunk1
at the position of the reference to chunk1.

The definition for the content of a chunk is the expanded content of all
blocks appended to the chunk concatenated.
The definition for the expanded content of a block is the content of the block
that all references to chunk are replaced by the content of the corresponding chunk.

It's a recursive procedure to evaluate the content of a chunk.
So a block can not includes the chunk that it extends.

The syntax above decribes some common concepts that all literate programming tools have.
litar has some useful extensions, which make it a general purpose tool
for text processing rather than a literate programming tool.
As a result, litar will not take in weaving to its kernel.

The major extension is user defined filters.
Noweb has some similar design decisions, but litar makes use of filters even more.

The following simple program explains the usage of filter in litar.

```
@@<hello.c@@=
#include "stdio.h"
int main() {
    @@<print hello world@@|exaggeratedly@@>
    return 0;
}
@@

@@<print hello world@@=
printf("Hello, world!\n");
@@

@@<exaggeratedly@@=#!/bin/sh
sed 's/world/WORLD/'
@@
```

The final output of this program is "Hello, WORLD!". The creation
of a chunk is pretty easy compared to files, so is the operation to
the chunk. The operation takes effect through pipes,
and itself is nothing more than a chunk. An operation like this is called filter.
Filter and pipe has same meaning as in the context of Unix shell.

Filters are scripts that the first line must be a shebang,
so litar can support all script languages without extra effort.

Filters can also make effect on blocks. The following program is same with the above.

```
@@<hello.c@@=
#include "stdio.h"
int main() {
    @@<print hello world@@>
    return 0;
}
@@

@@<print hello world@@=
printf("Hello, world!\n");
@@|exaggeratedly@@

@@<exaggeratedly@@=#!/bin/sh
sed 's/world/WORLD/'
@@
```

The two relations are extended to,
1. a chunk is extended by a block transformed by a filter;
2. a block include a chunk transformed by filters
   at the position of reference to the chunk.

The whole design of litar is around this extension, all other stuffs are
supplements to this, and this is the hardest part.
Filters are executed by litar, so litar has to
face the complication of building a correct process. For example,
which directory should be the working directory of filters,
what does it mean if a filter create a file,
how to decide what files a filter can access, etc.

It's ideal if a filter just use 3 files, stdin, stdout and stderr,
and do nothing more than transform input from stdin to stdout,
occasionally report error through stderr. In reality,
it's too restrictive. Like Bourne Shell, it can not do anything meaningful
without accessing tools provided by underlying operating system.
Even did we limit the choice of outside programs to a fixed set,
then how to explain, let's say, sed's ability to use script files.
So, to simply regard filters as executables and run them under
current directory is enough? No, what if the invocated sed command want
to make use of a script file written in the litar archive?
Should we extract the file from the litar archive manually then run the sed command?
It's too cumbersome, not at ease at all!

The author of litar does not want to limit the powerful flexibility of Unix tools.
So unlike traditional literate programming tools, litar has syntax to introduce
the concept of files explicitly to kernel of it. This is done by using @@[ and @@(.

```
@@[hello.c@@=
#include "stdio.h"
int main() {
    @@<print hello world@@|exaggeratedly@@>
    return 0;
}
@@

@@<print hello world@@=
printf("Hello, world!\n");
@@

@@[sed_scripts/bigger_world.sed@@=
s/world/WORLD/
@@

@@(run_bigger_world.sh@@=#!/bin/sh
sed -f sed_scripts/bigger_world.sed
@@

@@<exaggeratedly@@=#!/bin/sh
run_bigger_world.sh
@@
```

There are three files in this example, "hello.c", "sed_scripts/bigger_world.sed"
and "exaggeratedly". The difference between @@[ and @@( is that @@( specifies
a executable file.  Filters are executed in an environment so that these three
files can be visited.  A file in a litar archive is also a chunk.

Besides these control characters that describes the structure of a litar archive,
there are some other control characters. They are commands, which influence
the process of dealing with litar archive. For example, @@i receive an argument
of file name, and insert the content of that file at the place of @@i.
@

# Invocation

@<invocation syntax@=
{
    "name": "litar",
    "options": [
        @<command line options for litar@|remove ending comma@>
    ],
    "arguments": [
        "ARCHIVE"
    ]
}
@

@<manpage of litar@=
.TH LITAR 1
.SH NAME
litar \- an user space file system for litar archive
.SH SYNOPSIS
@<invocation syntax@|synopsis for manpage@>
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B litar
mount an user space file system based on a litar archive.
.SH OPTIONS
@<invocation syntax@|options for manpage@>
@

# Main body

litar follows the common pattern of a libfuse application,
parses and handles file system specific options first,
then just executes fuse_main.

@<litar.c@=
#define FUSE_MAJOR_VERSION 3
#define FUSE_MINOR_VERSION 14
#define FUSE_USE_VERSION FUSE_VERSION

@<includes@>
@<declarations@>
@<datatypes@>
@<flags@>
@<global variables@>
@<functions@>

static const struct fuse_operations litar_oper = {
@<operations for litar file system@>
};

int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
    struct fuse_args args = FUSE_ARGS_INIT(argc, argv);
    @<initializations of global variables@>
    @<parse command line arguments to set flags and global variables@>
    @<perform operations according to flags@>

    int fuse_argc;
    char **fuse_argv;
    @<construct "fuse_argc" and "fuse_argv"@>

    return fuse_main(fuse_argc, fuse_argv, &litar_oper, NULL);
}
@

@<functions@=
void print_usage()
{
    printf("Usage: %s\n", @<invocation syntax@|usage of invocation syntax@>);
}
@

@<global variables@=
char *archive_name = NULL;
@

@<parse command line arguments to set flags and global variables@=
do {
    struct stat buf;
    int opt;

    while (
        (
            opt = getopt(
                argc,
                argv,
                @<invocation syntax@|getopt string for invocation syntax@>
            )
        ) != -1
    ) {
        switch (opt) {
        @<cases for command line option@>
        default:
            print_usage();
            return 1;
        }
    }

    if (optind < argc) {
        archive_name = argv[optind];

        if (access(archive_name, R_OK) == -1) {
            perror(archive_name);
            return 1;
        }
    }
} while (0);
@

The operations before running fuse_main can be performed at two stages:

1. before parsing;
2. after parsing input.

@<perform operations according to flags@=
@<operations before parsing@|execute sequentially@>
@<parse input@>
@<operations after parsing input@|execute sequentially@>
@

litar does not require user to specify mount point manually.
By default, litar create a directory at work directory, and mount to that directory.
The directory's name is the litar archive's name removed suffix.
Users can also pass the mount point by command line argument "-m mountpoint".

@<command line options for litar@=
["specify the mount point", "m", "MOUNTPOINT"],
@

@<global variables@=
char *mountpoint;
@

@<cases for command line option@=
case 'm':
    mountpoint = optarg;
    break;
@

@<construct "fuse_argc" and "fuse_argv"@=
if (mountpoint == NULL) {
    char *dir = strdup(archive_name);
    int i = strlen(dir) - 1;
    
    while (dir[i] != '.' && i >= 0) {
        --i;
    }
    if (i > 0) {
        dir[i] = '\0';
    }
    if (mkdir(dir, 0755) == -1) {
        perror(dir);
        exit(1);
    }
    mountpoint = dir;
}
fuse_argc = 2;
CALLOC(fuse_argv, fuse_argc);
fuse_argv[0] = argv[0];
fuse_argv[1] = mountpoint;
@

# Concepts

Concepts illustrated above should be represented before details of implementation.

@<functions@=
@<relations between object@>
@

@<declarations@=
typedef struct mappings_chunk *Chunk;
typedef struct mappings_block *Block;
typedef struct mappings_chunk_ref *ChunkRef;
@

@<datatypes@=
struct mappings_chunk {
    @<mappings from chunk@>
};

struct mappings_block {
    @<mappings from block@>
};

struct mappings_chunk_ref {
    @<mappings from chunk ref@>
};
@

To save memory, reference to the original text is represented by some records
rather than stored to memory. The text can be later fetched through mmap in need.

@<datatypes@=
typedef struct {
    char *file;
    int start;
    int end;
} *TextRef;
@

@<global variables@=
HashTable file_to_mmap_addr = NULL;
@

@<initializations of global variables@=
new_string_hash_table(&file_to_mmap_addr);
@

@<functions@=
char *fetch_text_from(TextRef tr)
{
    void *addr;

    addr = get_from_hash_table(file_to_mmap_addr, tr->file);
    if (addr == NULL) {
        struct stat sbuf;
        int f;

        if ((f = open(tr->file, O_RDONLY)) < 0) {
            perror(tr->file);
            exit(1);
        }

        if (fstat(f, &sbuf) < 0) {
            perror(tr->file);
            exit(1);
        }

        addr = mmap(NULL, sbuf.st_size, PROT_READ, MAP_SHARED, f, 0);
        if (addr == MAP_FAILED) {
            perror(tr->file);
            exit(1);
        }
        put_to_hash_table(file_to_mmap_addr, tr->file, addr);
    }
    
    char *str = NULL;
    int len = tr->end - tr->start;
    CALLOC(str, len+1);
    memcpy(str, addr + tr->start, len);
    str[len] = '\0';
    return str;
}
@

All chunks have a name and
there's only one chunk if given a name. The name of a chunk
consists of none-space words, so "name of chunk" does not
differ from "  name  of  chunk".

@<declarations@=
void normalize_chunk_name(const char *orig, char *norm);
@

@<functions@=
void normalize_chunk_name(const char *orig, char *norm)
{
    int i = 0, j = 0, l = strlen(orig);
    while (i < l) {
        if (isspace(orig[i])) {
            if (j != 0 && norm[j-1] != ' ') {
                norm[j++] = ' ';
            }
        } else {
            norm[j++] = orig[i];
        }
        ++i;
    }
    if (norm[j-1] == ' ') {
        norm[j-1] = '\0';
    }
}
@

@<mappings from chunk@=
char *chunk_name;
@

@<global variables@=
HashTable chunk_name_to_chunk = NULL;
@

@<initializations of global variables@=
new_string_hash_table(&chunk_name_to_chunk);
@

@<relations between object@=
Chunk chunk_of_name(const char *name)
{
    char *norm;
    norm = strdup(name);
    normalize_chunk_name(name, norm);

    Chunk chk;
    chk = get_from_hash_table(chunk_name_to_chunk, norm);
    if (chk == NULL) {
        NEW0(chk);
        put_to_hash_table(chunk_name_to_chunk, norm, chk);
        chk->chunk_name = norm;
    } else {
        free(norm);
    }

    return chk;
}

bool chunk_of_name_exist(const char *name)
{
    char *norm;
    norm = strdup(name);
    normalize_chunk_name(name, norm);

    Chunk chk;
    chk = get_from_hash_table(chunk_name_to_chunk, norm);

    free(norm);
    return chk != NULL;
}

const char *name_of_chunk(Chunk chk)
{
    return chk->chunk_name;
}
@

Chunk is extended by a list of blocks transformed by filters.

@<mappings from chunk@=
List blocks;
@

@<mappings from block@=
Chunk chunk;
List filters;
@

@<relations between object@=
List blocks_of_chunk(Chunk chk)
{
    return chk->blocks;
}
void block_extend_chunk(Block blk, Chunk chk)
{
    List blks = blocks_of_chunk(chk);
    blks = cons(blk, blks);
    chk->blocks = blks;
    blk->chunk = chk;
}
List filters_of_block(Block blk)
{
    return blk->filters;
}
void block_is_transformed_by(Block blk, Chunk flt)
{
    push(flt, &blk->filters);
}
@

The contents of a block may either be a reference to the original text
or a reference to a chunk.

@<datatypes@=
typedef struct {
    enum {
        BLOCK_CONTENT_TEXT,
        BLOCK_CONTENT_CHUNK,
    } type;
    union {
        TextRef text;
        ChunkRef chunk;
    } value;
} *BlockContent;
@

A block contains a list of contents defined before.

@<mappings from block@=
List contents;
@

@<relations between object@=
List contents_of_block(Block blk)
{
    return blk->contents;
}

void block_contain_text(Block blk, TextRef tr)
{
    List bcs = contents_of_block(blk);
    BlockContent bc = NULL;

    NEW0(bc);
    bc->type = BLOCK_CONTENT_TEXT;
    bc->value.text = tr;

    bcs = cons(bc, bcs);
    blk->contents = bcs;
}

void block_include_chunk(Block blk, ChunkRef cr)
{
    List bcs = contents_of_block(blk);
    BlockContent bc = NULL;

    NEW0(bc);
    bc->type = BLOCK_CONTENT_CHUNK;
    bc->value.chunk = cr;

    bcs = cons(bc, bcs);
    blk->contents = bcs;
}
@

Sometimes it's useful to find the chunk the block extends.

@<relations between object@=
Chunk chunk_extended_by(Block blk)
{
    return blk->chunk;
}
@

A reference to chunk is consists of a chunk and a list of filters.

@<mappings from chunk ref@=
Chunk chunk;
List filters;
@

As said before, a filter is just a chunk.

@<relations between object@=
List filters_of_chunk_ref(ChunkRef ckr)
{
    return ckr->filters;
}

void chunk_ref_reference_to(ChunkRef ckr, Chunk chk)
{
    ckr->chunk = chk;
}

void chunk_ref_is_transformed_by(ChunkRef ckr, Chunk flt)
{
    push(flt, &ckr->filters);
}

Chunk chunk_of_chunk_ref(ChunkRef ckr)
{
    return ckr->chunk;
}
@

A litar archive is consists of comments and blocks, we call a comment or a block a section.
@<datatypes@=
typedef struct {
    enum {
        SECTION_COMMENT,
        SECTION_BLOCK,
    } type;
    union {
        TextRef comment;
        Block block;
    } content;
} *Section;
@

@<global variables@=
List sections;
@

Some chunks may be labeled as files,
a file system can be built from these file paths.

@<datatypes@=
typedef struct {
    enum {
        NODE_DIRECTORY,
        NODE_NORMAL,
        NODE_EXECUTABLE,
    } type;
    union {
        List nodes;
        Chunk chunk;
    } value;
    char *name;
} *Node;
@

@<global variables@=
Node root_node;
@

@<initializations of global variables@=
NEW(root_node);
root_node->type = NODE_DIRECTORY;
root_node->value.nodes = empty_list;
root_node->name = strdup("/");
@

@<relations between object@=
Node node_under_node(const char *file, Node node)
{
    Node nd;
    if (node->type != NODE_DIRECTORY) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Node %s is not directory\n", node->name);
        exit(1);
    }
    FOREACH(nd, node->value.nodes) {
        if (strcmp(file, nd->name) == 0) {
            return nd;
        }
    }
    return NULL;
}

Node node_of_path(const char *path)
{
    char *path_t = strdup(path);
    Node node = root_node;
    int i = 0, f = i, l = strlen(path);
    while (f < l) {
        if (path_t[i] == '/' || path_t[i] == '\0') {
            path_t[i] = '\0';
            if ((node = node_under_node(path_t+f, node)) == NULL) {
                break;
            }
            f = i+1;
        }
        ++i;
    }
    free(path_t);
    return node;
}

Node node_of_chunk(Chunk chk)
{
    return node_of_path(name_of_chunk(chk));
}

void chunk_is_file(Chunk chk)
{
    char *name = strdup(name_of_chunk(chk));
    Node node = root_node, nd;
    int i = 0, f = i, l = strlen(name);
    while (f < l) {
        if (name[i] == '/') {
            name[i] = '\0';
            if ((nd = node_under_node(name+f, node)) == NULL) {
                NEW(nd);
                nd->type = NODE_DIRECTORY;
                nd->name = strdup(name+f);
                nd->value.nodes = empty_list;
                push(nd, &node->value.nodes);
            }
            node = nd;
            f = i+1;
        }
        if (name[i] == '\0') {
            if ((nd = node_under_node(name+f, node)) == NULL) {
                NEW(nd);
                nd->type = NODE_NORMAL;
                nd->name = strdup(name+f);
                nd->value.chunk = chk;
                push(nd, &node->value.nodes);
            }
            break;
        }
        ++i;
    }
    free(name);
}

void chunk_is_executable(Chunk chk)
{
    chunk_is_file(chk);
    Node nd;

    nd = node_of_chunk(chk);
    nd->type = NODE_EXECUTABLE;
}
@

From the definitions above, we can define the content of chunk
and expanded content of block.
@<declarations@=
Str content_of_chunk(Chunk chk);
Str expanded_content_of_block(Block blk);
void transform(Str txt, List flts);
@

litar use a stack to detect recursive including and report it as a severe error.
@<global variables@=
List stack_of_chunks_in_tangling = empty_list;
@

@<functions@=
bool chunk_is_in_tangling(Chunk chk)
{
    Chunk chk_t;
    FOREACH(chk_t, stack_of_chunks_in_tangling) {
        if (chk_t == chk) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}
@

@<functions@=
Str content_of_chunk(Chunk chk)
{
    if (chunk_is_in_tangling(chk)) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Recursivly include \"%s\"\n", name_of_chunk(chk));
        exit(1);
    }

    Str cc = NULL;
    List blks = copy_list(blocks_of_chunk(chk));
    Block blk;

    reverse(&blks);

    push(chk, &stack_of_chunks_in_tangling);
    new_str(&cc, "");
    FOREACH(blk, blks) {
        Str exb = expanded_content_of_block(blk);
        transform(exb, blk->filters);
        str_extend(cc, raw_string(exb));
        free_str(&exb);
    }
    pop(&stack_of_chunks_in_tangling);

    free_list(&blks);
    return cc;
}

Str expanded_content_of_block(Block blk)
{
    Str ect = NULL;
    List bcs = copy_list(contents_of_block(blk));
    BlockContent bc;

    reverse(&bcs);
    
    new_str(&ect, "");
    FOREACH(bc, bcs) {
        if (bc->type == BLOCK_CONTENT_TEXT) {
            char *txt;
            txt = fetch_text_from(bc->value.text);
            str_extend(ect, txt);
            FREE(txt);
        }
        if (bc->type == BLOCK_CONTENT_CHUNK) {
            Str cc = content_of_chunk(bc->value.chunk->chunk);
            transform(cc, filters_of_chunk_ref(bc->value.chunk));
            str_extend(ect, raw_string(cc));
            free_str(&cc);
        }
    }
    
    free_list(&bcs);
    return ect;
}
@

# FUSE

@<functions@=
static void *litar_init(struct fuse_conn_info *conn, struct fuse_config *cfg)
{
    (void) conn;
    cfg->umask = 0222;
    return NULL;
}
static int litar_getattr(const char *path, struct stat *stbuf, struct fuse_file_info *fi)
{
    (void) fi;
    Node nd;

    memset(stbuf, 0, sizeof(struct stat));

    if (strcmp(path, "/") == 0) {
        stbuf->st_mode = S_IFDIR | 0555;
        stbuf->st_nlink = 2;
        return 0;
    }
    
    nd = node_of_path(path);
    if (nd == NULL) {
        return -ENOENT;
    }

    switch (nd->type) {
    case NODE_DIRECTORY:
    }
    return 0;
}
@

@<operations for litar file system@=
.init = litar_init,
@

@<functions@=
void transform(Str txt, List flts)
{
}
@

# Parsing

The purpose of parsing is to extract relations between objects of
a specific domain from unstructured information.

The grammar of litar is designed deliberately to be as simple as possible.
As a result, the tokens only have four types, control character,
normal character, newline and EOF,
and the whole parsing process can be implemented as a finite state machine.

@<datatypes@=
typedef struct {
    char *file;
    FILE *in;
    /* current position, current line, current row */
    int cp, cl, cr;
} *InputFrame;

typedef struct {
    enum {
        TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER,
        TOKEN_NORMAL_CHARACTER,
        TOKEN_NEWLINE,
        TOKEN_EOF,
    } type;
    char value;
} *Token;
@

@<functions@=
static Token next_token(InputFrame ipt)
{
    Token tkn = NULL;
    char c;
    int n = 0;

    NEW0(tkn);

    n += (c = fgetc(ipt->in)) == EOF ? 0 : 1;
    if (c == '\100') {
        tkn->type = TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER;
        n += (c = fgetc(ipt->in)) == EOF ? 0 : 1;
        if (c == EOF || c == '\n' || c == '\t' || c == ' ') {
            tkn->value = ' ';
        } else {
            tkn->value = c;
        }
        if (c == '\n') {
            n -= ungetc(c, ipt->in) == EOF ? 0 : 1;
        }
    } else if (c == '\n') {
        tkn->type = TOKEN_NEWLINE;
        tkn->value = '\n';
        ipt->cp += n;
        ++ipt->cl;
        ipt->cr = 0;
        n = 0;
    } else if (c == EOF) {
        tkn->type = TOKEN_EOF;
        tkn->value = EOF;
    } else {
        tkn->type = TOKEN_NORMAL_CHARACTER;
        tkn->value = c;
    }
    ipt->cp += n;
    ipt->cr += n;

    return tkn;
}
@

@<global variables@=
@<model of states@|declarations of states@>
@

@<model of states@=
[
    @<states of parsing@|remove ending comma@>
]
@

@<states of parsing@=
["parse", null, "XOR"],
@

@<parse input@=
do {
    @<model of states@|initialization of states@>

    @<model of states@|cleaning of states@>
} while (0);
@

## Top level

@<states of parsing@=
@

The parsing start with the state reading in comments.

@<states in a parsing@=
PARSE_COMMENT,
@

@<parse input (old)@=
do {
    State cs = PARSE_COMMENT; /* current state */
    List stack = empty_list;

    @<context for parsing@>

    do {
        InputFrame ipt = NULL;
        NEW0(ipt);
        ipt->file = strdup(archive_name);
        push(ipt, &stack);
    } while (0);

    while (!is_empty_list(stack)) {
        Token tkn;
        InputFrame ipt;

        RENAME(car(stack), ipt);

        if (ipt->cp == 0) {
            if ((ipt->in = fopen(ipt->file, "r")) == NULL) {
                perror(ipt->file);
                return 1;
            }
        }

        int cp_t, cl_t, cr_t;

        cp_t = ipt->cp;
        cl_t = ipt->cl;
        cr_t = ipt->cr;

        tkn = next_token(ipt);
        switch (cs) {
        @<cases for handling token "tkn"@>
        }

        FREE(tkn);
    }
} while (0);
@

@<functions@=
void handle_unknown_token(Token tkn, char *f, int l, int r)
{
    fprintf(stderr,
        "Unexpected token at line %d row %d of %s\n",
        l, r, f
    );
    exit(1);
}
@

@<execute token handlings@=#!/bin/sh
echo "do {"
cat -
echo "handle_unknown_token(tkn, ipt->file, cl_t, cr_t);"
echo "} while (0);"
@

@<cases for handling token "tkn"@=
case PARSE_COMMENT:
    @<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "PARSE_COMMENT"@|execute token handlings@>
    break;
@

@<context for parsing@=
TextRef ctxt = NULL;
Section csec = NULL;

NEW0(ctxt);
ctxt->file = archive_name;

NEW0(csec);
csec->type = SECTION_COMMENT;
csec->content.comment = ctxt;
push(csec, &sections);
@

@<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "PARSE_COMMENT"@=
if (tkn->type == TOKEN_NORMAL_CHARACTER || tkn->type == TOKEN_NEWLINE) {
    ctxt->end = ipt->cp;
    break;
}
@

So character @@ should also be able to be input.
@<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "PARSE_COMMENT"@=
if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '\100') {
    csec = NULL;
    NEW0(csec);
    csec->type = SECTION_COMMENT;
    push(csec, &sections);

    ctxt = NULL;
    NEW0(ctxt);
    ctxt->file = ipt->file;
    ctxt->start = cp_t+1;
    ctxt->end = cp_t+1;
    csec->content.comment = ctxt;
    break;
}
@

End parsing of currnet file.
@<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "PARSE_COMMENT"@=
if (tkn->type == TOKEN_EOF) {
    fclose(ipt->in);
    FREE(ipt);
    pop(&stack);
    break;
}
@

@<states in a parsing@=
PARSE_BLOCK_CHUNK_NAME,
PARSE_BLOCK_CONTENTS,
PARSE_BLOCK_FILTER,
@

@<cases for handling token "tkn"@=
case PARSE_BLOCK_CHUNK_NAME:
    @<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "PARSE_BLOCK_CHUNK_NAME"@|execute token handlings@>
    break;
case PARSE_BLOCK_CONTENTS:
    @<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "PARSE_BLOCK_CONTENTS"@|execute token handlings@>
    break;
case PARSE_BLOCK_FILTER:
    @<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "PARSE_BLOCK_FILTER"@|execute token handlings@>
    break;
@

@<context for parsing@=
Block cblk = NULL;
Str cache = NULL;
@

@<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "PARSE_COMMENT"@=
if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '<') {
    ctxt = NULL;
    csec = NULL;

    NEW0(csec);
    csec->type = SECTION_BLOCK;
    push(csec, &sections);

    NEW0(cblk);
    csec->content.block = cblk;

    new_str(&cache, "");
    cs = PARSE_BLOCK_CHUNK_NAME;
    break;
}
@

Read in chunk name.
@<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "PARSE_BLOCK_CHUNK_NAME"@=
if (tkn->type == TOKEN_NORMAL_CHARACTER || tkn->type == TOKEN_NEWLINE) {
    str_append(cache, tkn->value);
    break;
}

if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '\100') {
    str_append(cache, '\100');
    break;
}

if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '=') {
    char *chkn;
    Chunk chk;

    chkn = raw_string(cache);
    chk = chunk_of_name(chkn);
    block_extend_chunk(cblk, chk);
    
    free_str(&cache);

    NEW0(ctxt);
    ctxt->file = ipt->file;
    ctxt->start = ipt->cp;
    ctxt->end = ipt->cp;

    block_contain_text(cblk, ctxt);

    cs = PARSE_BLOCK_CONTENTS;
    break;
}
@

@<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "PARSE_BLOCK_CONTENTS"@=
if (tkn->type == TOKEN_NORMAL_CHARACTER || tkn->type == TOKEN_NEWLINE) {
    ++ctxt->end;
    break;
}

if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '\100') {
    ctxt = NULL;
    NEW0(ctxt);
    ctxt->file = ipt->file;
    ctxt->start = cp_t+1;
    ctxt->end = cp_t+1;
    block_contain_text(cblk, ctxt);
    break;
}

if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '|') {
    ctxt = NULL;
    new_str(&cache, "");
    cs = PARSE_BLOCK_FILTER;
    break;
}

if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == ' ') {
    cblk = NULL;

    ctxt = NULL;
    NEW0(ctxt);
    ctxt->file = ipt->file;
    ctxt->start = ipt->cp;
    ctxt->end = ipt->cp;

    csec = NULL;
    NEW0(csec);
    csec->type = SECTION_COMMENT;
    csec->content.comment = ctxt;
    push(csec, &sections);
    cs = PARSE_COMMENT;
    break;
}
@

@<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "PARSE_BLOCK_FILTER"@=
if (tkn->type == TOKEN_NORMAL_CHARACTER || tkn->type == TOKEN_NEWLINE) {
    str_append(cache, tkn->value);
    break;
}

if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '|') {
    char *chkn;
    Chunk chk;

    chkn = raw_string(cache);
    chk = chunk_of_name(chkn);
    block_is_transformed_by(cblk, chk);
    free_str(&cache);

    new_str(&cache, "");
    break;
}

if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == ' ') {
    char *chkn;
    Chunk chk;

    chkn = raw_string(cache);
    chk = chunk_of_name(chkn);
    block_is_transformed_by(cblk, chk);
    free_str(&cache);

    cblk = NULL;

    NEW0(ctxt);
    ctxt->file = ipt->file;
    ctxt->start = ipt->cp;
    ctxt->end = ipt->cp;

    csec = NULL;
    NEW0(csec);
    csec->type = SECTION_COMMENT;
    csec->content.comment = ctxt;
    push(csec, &sections);
    cs = PARSE_COMMENT;
    break;
}
@

@<states in a parsing@=
PARSE_REFERENCE_CHUNK_NAME,
@

@<context for parsing@=
ChunkRef cckr = NULL;
@

@<cases for handling token "tkn"@=
case PARSE_REFERENCE_CHUNK_NAME:
    @<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "PARSE_REFERENCE_CHUNK_NAME"@|execute token handlings@>
    break;
@

@<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "PARSE_BLOCK_CONTENTS"@=
if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '<') {
    ctxt = NULL;
    new_str(&cache, "");
    NEW0(cckr);
    block_include_chunk(cblk, cckr);
    cs = PARSE_REFERENCE_CHUNK_NAME;
    break;
}
@

@<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "PARSE_REFERENCE_CHUNK_NAME"@=
if (tkn->type == TOKEN_NORMAL_CHARACTER || tkn->type == TOKEN_NEWLINE) {
    str_append(cache, tkn->value);
    break;
}

if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '\100') {
    str_append(cache, '\100');
    break;
}

if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '>') {
    char *chkn;
    Chunk chk;

    chkn = raw_string(cache);
    chk = chunk_of_name(chkn);
    free_str(&cache);

    chunk_ref_reference_to(cckr, chk);
    cckr = NULL;

    NEW0(ctxt);
    ctxt->file = ipt->file;
    ctxt->start = ipt->cp;
    ctxt->end = ipt->cp;
    block_contain_text(cblk, ctxt);
    cs = PARSE_BLOCK_CONTENTS;
    break;
}
@

@<states in a parsing@=
PARSE_REFERENCE_FILTER,
@

@<cases for handling token "tkn"@=
case PARSE_REFERENCE_FILTER:
    @<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "PARSE_REFERENCE_FILTER"@|execute token handlings@>
    break;
@

@<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "PARSE_REFERENCE_CHUNK_NAME"@=
if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '|') {
    char *chkn;
    Chunk chk;

    chkn = raw_string(cache);
    chk = chunk_of_name(chkn);
    free_str(&cache);

    chunk_ref_reference_to(cckr, chk);

    new_str(&cache, "");
    cs = PARSE_REFERENCE_FILTER;
    break;
}
@

@<handlings for token "tkn" when state is "PARSE_REFERENCE_FILTER"@=
if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '|') {
    if (cache != NULL) {
        char *chkn;
        Chunk chk;

        chkn = raw_string(cache);
        chk = chunk_of_name(chkn);
        free_str(&cache);

        chunk_ref_is_transformed_by(cckr, chk);
    }
    new_str(&cache, "");
    break;
}

if (tkn->type == TOKEN_NORMAL_CHARACTER || tkn->type == TOKEN_NEWLINE) {
    str_append(cache, tkn->value);
    break;
}

if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '\100') {
    str_append(cache, '\100');
    break;
}

if (tkn->type == TOKEN_CONTROL_CHARACTER && tkn->value == '>') {
    char *chkn;
    Chunk chk;

    chkn = raw_string(cache);
    chk = chunk_of_name(chkn);
    free_str(&cache);

    chunk_ref_is_transformed_by(cckr, chk);
    cckr = NULL;

    NEW0(ctxt);
    ctxt->file = ipt->file;
    ctxt->start = ipt->cp;
    ctxt->end = ipt->cp;
    block_contain_text(cblk, ctxt);
    cs = PARSE_BLOCK_CONTENTS;
    break;
}
@

# User Interface

@<command line options for litar@=
["Show the usage of litar.", "h"],
@

@<flags@=
bool to_print_help;
@

@<cases for command line option@=
case 'h':
    to_print_help = true;
    break;
@

@<operations before parsing@=
if (to_print_help) {
    print_usage();
    exit(0);
}
@

@<command line options for litar@=
["Show the version and build information of litar.", "v"],
@

@<flags@=
bool to_print_version_info;
@

@<cases for command line option@=
case 'v':
    to_print_version_info = true;
    break;
@

@<global variables@=
char version[] = "dev";
@

@<operations before parsing@=
if (to_print_version_info) {
    @<print version information@|fill in build time information@>
    exit(0);
}
@

@<print version information@=
printf("Version %s, built at <build time>\nlibfuse version: %d\n", version, FUSE_VERSION);
@

@<command line options for litar@=
["Print the content of CHUNK in ARCHIVE.", "p", "CHUNK"],
["Execute an operation in CHUNK under ARCHIVE.", "x", "CHUNK"],
@

@<global variables@=
char *name_of_chunk_to_print;
@

@<flags@=
bool to_print_specific_chunk;
@

@<cases for command line option@=
case 'p':
    to_print_specific_chunk = true;
    name_of_chunk_to_print = optarg;
    break;
@

@<operations after parsing input@=
if (to_print_specific_chunk) {
    if (!chunk_of_name_exist(name_of_chunk_to_print)) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Unknown chunk: %s\n", name_of_chunk_to_print);
        exit(1);
    }
    Str cc;
    cc = content_of_chunk(chunk_of_name(name_of_chunk_to_print));
    puts(raw_string(cc));
    free_str(&cc);
    break;
}
@<print all sections@>
@

@<functions@=
void print_section(Section sec)
{
    if (sec->type == SECTION_COMMENT) {
        printf("Comment: (%s, %d, %d)\nContent:\n%s\n",
            sec->content.comment->file,
            sec->content.comment->start,
            sec->content.comment->end,
            fetch_text_from(sec->content.comment)
        );
    } else {
        printf("Block: %s\n", name_of_chunk(sec->content.block->chunk));
        List lst = copy_list(contents_of_block(sec->content.block));
        reverse(&lst);
        do {
            BlockContent bc;
            FOREACH(bc, lst) {
                if (bc->type == BLOCK_CONTENT_TEXT) {
                    printf("\tText: (%s, %d, %d)\n",
                        bc->value.text->file,
                        bc->value.text->start,
                        bc->value.text->end
                    );
                } else {
                    printf("\tReference: %s", name_of_chunk(bc->value.chunk->chunk));
                    List lst2 = copy_list(bc->value.chunk->filters);
                    reverse(&lst2);
                    Chunk chk;
                    FOREACH(chk, lst2) {
                        printf(" | %s", name_of_chunk(chk));
                    }
                    free_list(&lst2);
                    putchar('\n');
                }
            }
        } while (0);
        free_list(&lst);
    }
}
@

@<print all sections@=
do {
    Section sec;
    reverse(&sections);
    FOREACH(sec, sections) {
        print_section(sec);
    }
} while (0);
@

